{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 67, :comments ["<b>And Isaac brought her into the tent of Sarah his mother.</b> The nismach (the word to which it is linked) is missing, and there are many like this. The reason of the verse is that it tells of the honor that Isaac showed his mother, for from the time that Sarah died, no one had spread her tent, for they said, no other woman shall enter the tent of the esteemed matriarch. And when he saw Rebekah, he brought her into that tent in her honor, and there he took her. This is the reason of \"and he loved her and was comforted\" — it hints that he had been deeply grieved over his mother and far removed from any comforter, until he was comforted in his wife through his love for her. For what reason would the verse mention a man's love for his wife? And Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 24:67) explained: \"And Isaac brought her into the tent, and behold, it was Sarah his mother\" — therefore he mentioned the love, for because of her righteousness and the excellence of her deeds he loved her and was comforted through her. And thus they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 60:16): until Sarah died, blessing was found in the dough."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:34:31.356793828Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}